+# smallpt in the browser
+
+Kevin Beason's [smallpt](https://www.kevinbeason.com/smallpt/) C++ path tracer, compiled to WebAssembly with Emscripten. Open `index.html` through your static host. All tracing happens locally in a module Web Worker; no rendering service, GPU API, or cross-origin isolation is required.
+
+The page starts a progressive render automatically. Randomize the two sphere tints, choose colors manually, adjust resolution and sample count, rerender, reset, expand the canvas, or download the current image as PNG. A new request cancels the previous render. The bundled preview was rendered with the same C++ adapter at 256 samples per pixel and remains visible until the first live pass arrives.
+
+## Rebuild
+
+Install Emscripten and make `emcc` available, then run:
+
+```sh
+npm run build
+```
+
+The build regenerates `assets/smallpt.js` and `assets/smallpt.wasm`. These files are checked in so the static site works without a local compiler or build step.
+
+`src/smallpt-original.cpp` preserves the upstream source. `src/smallpt.cpp` keeps its scene, ray intersections, material models, and radiance recursion, replacing the command-line loop and PPM output with progressive accumulation and an RGBA buffer. OpenMP is omitted because work runs in a browser worker. Samples accumulate linearly before display clamping and gamma conversion.
+
+Upstream MIT license and attribution are retained in `src/LICENSE.txt`.
